<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Manyika</h1><p class="st-meta">1490 CE – 1891 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Shona kingdom of the eastern highlands, ruled by the Mutasa, whose goldfields drew Portuguese traders and later British and Portuguese rivals.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Manyika emerged around 1490 in the highlands that now straddle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>–<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Mozambique</a> border, as a tributary state on the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> realm. Its rulers, who bore the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutasa">Mutasa</a>, soon became effectively independent, controlling the alluvial gold of the region and the routes to the coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutasa">Mutasa</a> ruled through a network of chiefs and kin, and rainmaking and ancestral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spirit-medium">spirit mediums</a> supported royal authority. Gold was traded at Portuguese fairs such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manica-mozambique">Masekesa</a>, and the kingdom was a frequent target of Portuguese, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-barwe">Barwe</a> and Uteve ambitions. In the 19th century it suffered raids from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaza-empire">Gaza Nguni</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1890 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutasa">Mutasa</a> signed an agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a>, which clashed with the Portuguese claim. Fighting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manica-mozambique">Macequece</a> in 1891 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-portuguese-treaty-of-1891">Anglo-Portuguese treaty</a> that year split Manyika between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-rhodesia">Southern Rhodesia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a>. Manyika remains a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> dialect and regional identity.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1490 CE – 1891 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.35%;width:7.98%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1490 CE – 1891 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Shona religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shona (Manyika)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, <a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Manica, Manyika</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1690&amp;focus=kingdom-of-manyika" aria-label="Kingdom of Manyika on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5490 2879 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Manyika lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5833 3185h0M5791 3172h0M5941 3168h0M5842 3096h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5810 3135h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5810 3135h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Zimbabwe. <a href="/atlas?year=1690&amp;focus=kingdom-of-manyika">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
